<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French National Committee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Comité national français</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French National Committee was the governing body of Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in September 1941. Its members held portfolios like ministers, and it gained growing recognition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> as the representative of fighting France. In June 1943 it merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a>'s command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-national-committee.jpg" alt="French National Committee" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author (Keystone-France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comit%C3%A9_national_fran%C3%A7ais.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
